# Merge strategies to stop lock / append-only files from hand-conflicting
# on every branch when many agents fan out from a fast-moving main (#1004).
#
# `union` is git's BUILT-IN low-level merge driver: on a conflicting hunk
# it keeps both sides' lines instead of raising a conflict. Being built in,
# it needs no per-clone `git config` and takes effect for every worktree
# the moment this file is committed.
#
# Only files where a line-wise union is *safe* live here. Generated
# artifacts compiled from Rust source (apps/desktop/src/generated/*, the
# keymap-docs reference, protocol fingerprint) are deliberately NOT listed:
# a merge driver runs mid-merge, before the merged source reaches the
# working tree, so it would regenerate from the un-merged side — see
# scripts/rebase-onto-main.sh, which regenerates them *after* the merge
# stops instead. `.snap` snapshots are excluded too: a union of two
# regenerated snapshots is malformed, and a visible conflict is the safer
# signal to re-accept.

# Any branch touching a dependency re-resolves Cargo.lock, so two branches
# adding crates collide constantly. Union keeps both sides' lines; the next
# `cargo build`/`cargo update` normalises the merged lockfile.
Cargo.lock merge=union

# The shared snippet catalog is append-only — each snippet is a distinct
# entry under `snippets:`. Union keeps every branch's additions rather than
# conflicting on adjacent inserts.
.lazybox/snippets.yaml merge=union
